{"id":"h169","run":2,"role":"calibration","category":"science-physics","question":"If a normal eye cannot separate two objects (or images) subtending an angle of less than 1.5 minutes, approximate the magnifying power that is necessary to make use (visually) of the full resolving power of a telescope whose objective has a diameter of 40 inches. (Assume a mean wavelength of 5.5 × 10^-5 cm.)\nIs the correct answer \"750\"?","truth":"no","model_answer":"no","correct":true,"source":"TIGER-Lab/MMLU-Pro, test split","claims":[{"text":"The correct magnification is about 800, not 750.","confidence":0.9,"lean":-0.9,"theta":1.4137,"phi":-3.0888},{"text":"The telescope's diffraction-limited resolution is approximately λ/D, about 5.4e-7 rad (0.11 arcsec).","confidence":0.85,"lean":-0.5,"theta":1.3352,"phi":-2.7611},{"text":"The eye's resolution limit of 1.5 arcmin is about 4.36e-4 rad.","confidence":0.9,"lean":0.0,"theta":1.4137,"phi":-2.9236},{"text":"The required magnification is the eye's limit divided by the telescope's resolution, which is about 800.","confidence":0.85,"lean":-0.8,"theta":1.3352,"phi":-2.9198},{"text":"750 could be acceptable as an approximate value in the same range as the estimate.","confidence":0.2,"lean":0.6,"theta":0.3142,"phi":0.032},{"text":"The direct calculation gives about 806, which rounds to roughly 800.","confidence":0.8,"lean":-0.8,"theta":1.2566,"phi":-2.9293},{"text":"Using the Rayleigh criterion (1.22λ/D) gives about 660, which is also not 750.","confidence":0.7,"lean":-0.6,"theta":1.0996,"phi":1.4328},{"text":"No standard choice of constants yields 750.","confidence":0.7,"lean":-0.8,"theta":1.0996,"phi":3.1383}],"relations":[{"i":0,"j":3,"J":0.9,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":4,"J":-0.8,"relation":"contradicts"},{"i":0,"j":5,"J":0.9,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":6,"J":0.2,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":7,"J":0.6,"relation":"supports"},{"i":1,"j":3,"J":0.6,"relation":"supports"},{"i":1,"j":5,"J":0.4,"relation":"supports"},{"i":1,